      "acceptable_supporting": [
        "least-privilege"
      ]
    },
    "description": "HarborPay lets treasury specialists create wire disbursements above 25,000 USD, but releasing those wires is a separate sensitive action. A specialist who creates a payment must not be able to approve or release that same payment, even by signing in through another browser or using an administrator account. The Payments Console records the creator, the approver, and the releaser separately; the Workflow Service rejects a release when the approving identity equals the creating identity, and a second authorized treasury specialist must approve before the Release Adapter can contact the banking partner. Service accounts used by the console and release adapter are not permitted to stand in for either human reviewer, and automated reconciliation cannot release a pending payment. If the approval record cannot be retrieved or its signer cannot be verified, the payment remains pending. The ledger keeps the full history of both human actions and prevents either reviewer from editing the other reviewer's decision.",
    "decision_statement": "Should a wire above the threshold require approval and release by different treasury specialists before any funds can be sent?",
    "notes": "Wires above threshold require creation, approval, and release by different treasury specialists (separation-of-duties); fail-safe-defaults is the near-miss via payments staying pending when approval cannot be verified, with least-privilege supporting the service-account restrictions."
  },
